 rows: [
 ['Editing flexibility', 'More latitude for exposure and white balance adjustments; recovery still has limits.', 'Can be edited, but strong adjustments can reveal artifacts or lost detail.'],
 ['File size', 'Usually larger; varies with camera, scene and compression mode.', 'Usually smaller; quality settings and scene detail affect size.'],
 ['Sharing & software', 'Usually needs a compatible editor and export for sharing.', 'Widely supported and ready to share straight from the camera.'],
 ['Burst shooting', 'Larger files may fill the buffer sooner; check your camera.', 'Smaller files can help sustain longer bursts; camera settings matter.'],
 ['Best fit', 'Editing, difficult lighting and keeping a flexible original.', 'Fast delivery, simple workflows and little editing.']
 ],
 sub: 'Should you shoot RAW, JPEG or both?',
 choices: [['Planning to edit?', 'Choose RAW for more control over your final image.'], ['Sharing right away?', 'Choose JPEG when a finished image and quick delivery matter most.'], ['Need both?', 'Choose RAW + JPEG for a shareable image plus an editable original; allow more space.']],
 note: 'A RAW file is not automatically a better-looking photo. The editor, camera settings and processing affect the result. Neither format can recover information that was never captured.', source: 'Further reading: Nikon’s guide to choosing RAW or JPEG.'
